  <p>Climate change is not a distant threat for Nauru — it is an immediate and accelerating reality. With a maximum elevation of just 65 metres and the majority of the population and critical infrastructure concentrated along a narrow coastal belt, the island is exceptionally vulnerable to rising sea levels, intensified storm surges, and coastal erosion. Nauru's climate response is set out in two primary documents: its updated Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) covering 2025–2035, and the GEF-backed CARE4N coastal resilience project.</p>

  <h2>What this means</h2>
  <p>Nauru's adaptation effort has two distinct tracks. The first is <strong>coastal protection</strong> — defending the shoreline where critical infrastructure sits. The second is <strong>longer-term resilience planning</strong>, which for a nation of roughly 21 square kilometres necessarily includes difficult questions about how much coastline can realistically be defended, and what happens to the areas that cannot.</p>
  <p>For visitors, the practical impact is mostly localised: coastal works can restrict beach access and cause minor disruption, and conditions change over time. The island's fundamentals — limited infrastructure, few services, and a genuinely off-the-beaten-track character — remain the same.</p>

  <h2>Key projects and frameworks</h2>
  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>🌊 CARE4N — Coastal Area Resilience Enhancement for Nauru</h4>
    <p>Nauru's most concrete coastal protection effort is CARE4N, supported through the Global Environment Facility (GEF) with the Asian Development Bank as implementing agency. The project's objective is that Nauru's coastal assets and ecosystems enjoy improved protection in an integrated, affordable, sustainable, adaptive, equitable, and replicable manner. Funding comprises a GEF allocation of <strong>USD 2,660,600</strong> together with <strong>USD 1,000,000</strong> in co-financing. Nauru's own climate documentation notes that CARE4N will not cover the full cost of implementing all recommended hard and nature-based solutions — it is a starting point rather than a complete coastal defence programme.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>📄 Updated Nationally Determined Contribution (2025–2035)</h4>
    <p>Nauru has submitted an updated NDC covering 2025 to 2035 under the Paris Agreement, published in full on the UNFCCC website. The NDC is the authoritative statement of what Nauru itself commits to and requests internationally — a more reliable basis for understanding national climate policy than secondary reporting, because it is the document Nauru formally submits to the international process.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>🏔️ Managed Relocation and the Topside</h4>
    <p>Alongside engineered coastal protection, Nauru's adaptation planning has included discussion of managed relocation of people and assets toward the island's higher interior — the Topside phosphate plateau. This reflects the reality that in some locations, defending the coastline may not be feasible indefinitely. The Topside also offers the island's most dramatic landscapes and is worth visiting for its own sake.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <h4>🌱 Nature-Based Solutions</h4>
    <p>Across Pacific island states, adaptation measures commonly include natural coastal buffers such as native vegetation and coral rubble berms, alongside engineered structures. Nauru's specific mix of measures is set out in its NDC and project documentation rather than in secondary summaries.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why this matters for your visit</h2>
  <p>Climate adaptation work has practical implications for anyone travelling to Nauru:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coastal works</strong> can cause localised access restrictions and minor traffic disruption — ask at your accommodation about current conditions</li>
    <li><strong>Beach access</strong> at some points may be affected by works; conditions change, so check locally rather than relying on older guides</li>
    <li><strong>Services and infrastructure</strong> on Nauru are limited by island scale — this is a constant, not a temporary condition, and planning around it is part of visiting the island</li>
    <li><strong>The Topside plateau</strong> remains accessible and is one of the island's most striking features, regardless of coastal works elsewhere</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Nauru remains a destination where conditions are best confirmed locally on arrival. Treat any travel guide — including this one — as a starting point, not a substitute for current local information.</p>

  <h2>Where to verify this information</h2>
  <p>The primary sources for this article are the Global Environment Facility's project page for CARE4N (project ID 11705) and Nauru's Updated Nationally Determined Contribution 2025–2035, published on the UNFCCC website.</p>
  <p><strong>Correction note:</strong> An earlier version of this page described a "2026 Climate Resilience Agreement with the Pacific Island Forum's $500 million Adaptation Fund" providing "an $18 million allocation" to Nauru, including a "3.2-kilometre engineered revetment wall," a "3 MW solar installation," and a "6 MWh battery storage system." Those figures could not be verified against any official source and have been removed.</p>

